/* Cert Gauntlet — SC-200 cards, domain 2: Respond to security incidents */
export const SC200_CARDS_D2 = [
 /* ---------- xdrresp (80) ---------- */
 /* 2.1.1 Defender for Office 365 */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"Threat Explorer vs Real-time detections — licensing split",a:"#Explorer needs Defender for Office 365 Plan 2; Real-time detections ships with Plan 1\nExplorer adds the All email, Campaigns and URL clicks views plus post-delivery history",s:"Plan 2 email hunting tool; Plan 1 gets Real-time detections",g:"dfo365tools",
  x:"Both tools live under Email and collaboration in the Defender portal and show detections at delivery time. Explorer is the Plan 2 superset: it also tracks what happened after delivery (ZAP, admin actions, user clicks), offers more filters and saved queries. Stem clue: a question that says the tenant only has Plan 1 and asks why the Campaigns view or All email view is missing is pointing at this licensing line."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"Soft delete vs hard delete on an email in Threat Explorer",a:"#Soft delete parks the message in Recoverable Items\\Deletions; hard delete purges it\nSoft-deleted mail is recoverable by user and admin; hard-deleted only by admin single-item recovery",s:"Recoverable Items move versus an admin-only purge",g:"dfo365action",
  x:"The Take action wizard on selected messages offers Move to Junk, Move to Inbox, Move to Deleted Items, Soft delete and Hard delete. Soft delete is the usual remediation because it is reversible by the recipient, while hard delete removes the item from the user's reach entirely. Expect a scenario where the SOC must remove a phish but keep it recoverable for a later eDiscovery review: soft delete is the answer.",
  w:"Soft = user can still recover; hard = only an admin can."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"Permission needed to move or delete email from Explorer",a:"#Search and Purge role (Email and collaboration) or the unified RBAC equivalent\nUnified RBAC: Security operations / Security data / Email and collaboration advanced actions (manage)",s:"Search and Purge role, or its unified RBAC permission",
  x:"Reading detections in Explorer is open to security readers, but moving or purging mail is a destructive action gated by Search and Purge. Out of the box only the Data Investigator and Organization Management role groups carry it, so a question about an analyst who can see a phishing message but cannot delete it is asking for that role. Preview and download of message content needs the separate Preview role."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"Two-step approval for email remediation — how it flows",a:"#An analyst proposes a soft delete; an approver confirms it in the Action center\nPending actions sit on the Action center Pending tab until approved or rejected",s:"Proposed remediation waits in the Action center for approval",g:"dfo365action",
  x:"When the person submitting an email action lacks purge rights, or when AIR recommends an action, the remediation is created as pending. Someone with Search and Purge opens the Action center (Actions and submissions) and approves or rejects it; the History tab then records the outcome. The exam tends to describe a junior analyst whose email deletion never happens and asks where the request is waiting."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"Zero-hour auto purge (ZAP) — what it does",a:"#Retroactively moves already-delivered mail after a verdict changes\nLooks back 48 hours; malware and high-confidence phish go to quarantine",s:"Pulls back delivered mail once it is judged malicious",g:"zap",
  x:"ZAP runs continuously in Exchange Online: when signatures or intelligence later classify a delivered message as malware, phishing or spam, it moves the message without user notification. Malware and high-confidence phishing are quarantined regardless of read state, while spam ZAP only touches unread mail and follows the anti-spam policy action. In Explorer, filter the All email view by Additional action equal to ZAP to find those events.",
  w:"ZAP = the mail server changes its mind within 48 hours."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"ZAP for spam vs ZAP for high-confidence phishing — the difference",a:"#Spam ZAP acts only on unread messages and obeys the anti-spam policy action\nHigh-confidence phish and malware are quarantined whether read or not",s:"Spam only if unread; phish and malware even if read",g:"zap",
  x:"The policy action matters: if the anti-spam policy says Move to Junk, spam ZAP moves to Junk; if the action is add X-header, prepend subject, redirect or delete, ZAP does nothing for that verdict. Malware and high-confidence phishing bypass that logic and are quarantined. A stem asking why a read spam message survived while a read phish was pulled is testing this distinction.",
  w:"Read mail survives spam ZAP, not phish ZAP."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"Campaign views in Defender for Office 365 — what they add",a:"#Groups related phish or malware mail into a coordinated attack with a Campaign ID\nFlow diagram: sender IPs, domains, filter verdicts, destinations, URL clicks",s:"Coordinated attack grouping with a flow diagram and an ID",g:"dfo365tools",
  x:"Campaigns (Plan 2 only) cluster messages by sender infrastructure, payload and style so an analyst sees the whole wave instead of single messages. The flyout shows Targeted percentage, Inboxed, Clicked and Visited counts, and the Explore messages button pivots into Threat Explorer with filters applied. On the exam, a request to see how many users clicked across an entire coordinated phishing wave is a Campaigns answer.",
  w:"One campaign = many messages, one ID, one Sankey flow."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"Automated investigation and response (AIR) in Defender for Office 365 — triggers",a:"#Alert policies such as user-reported phish, ZAP removals and a URL click verdict turning malicious\nCan also be started manually from Explorer or the email entity page",s:"Started by email alerts or manually from Explorer",g:"dfo365tools",
  x:"AIR (Plan 2) examines the message, its URLs and attachments, the sender and the affected users, then produces recommended actions such as soft delete, block URL or disable a user. Every recommendation waits for approval in the Action center except auto-remediation of malicious URL and file clusters. Exam signal: 'an investigation was triggered automatically after a user reported a message' is AIR, not attack disruption."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"Attack disruption for business email compromise — the identity actions",a:"#Disables or contains the compromised sender so fraud mail stops in minutes\nIncident gets the Attack Disruption tag and a yellow banner naming the action",s:"Automatic user disable or containment on a BEC incident",
  x:"When Defender XDR correlates signals into a high-confidence BEC incident, attack disruption can disable the user in Active Directory, suspend them in Entra ID, revoke sessions or contain the user on endpoints without waiting for an analyst. The incident title carries the attack disruption suffix via the API and the tag in the queue. Every automatic action is listed in the Action center and can be undone once the SOC confirms the account is clean.",
  w:"Disruption stops the account; the SOC reviews afterwards."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.1",q:"Submit to Microsoft from Explorer — purpose",a:"#Reports a message, URL or attachment as clean, suspicious or malicious\nCan also add Tenant Allow/Block List entries; needs no purge role",s:"Report a verdict to Microsoft, optionally add allow/block",g:"dfo365action",
  x:"Submissions tell Microsoft that a filter verdict was wrong, feeding back into detection, and they are available even in Plan 1 without Search and Purge. From the same wizard an analyst can add senders, domains, URLs or file hashes to the Tenant Allow/Block List. Distinguish from user-reported messages, which arrive on the Submissions page's User reported tab and can themselves trigger AIR."},
 /* 2.1.2 Purview */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.2",q:"Purview DLP alerts in the Defender portal — how to find them",a:"#Filter the incident queue by service source Microsoft Data Loss Prevention\nFlows automatically for eligible E5-class tenants",s:"Incident queue filtered to the Data Loss Prevention source",
  x:"When the tenant holds Microsoft 365 E5, E5 Compliance or E5 Information Protection and Governance, DLP policy matches generate alerts inside Defender XDR alongside endpoint and email alerts. The alert page shows the matched policy, the sensitive information types, the user and the file or message. Exam phrasing: 'the SOC must triage data-loss events without leaving the Defender portal' points here rather than to the Purview DLP alerts dashboard."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.2",q:"DLP alert page in Defender — the evidence it exposes",a:"#Matched policy, sensitive info types tab, user, file link and Source tab for content\nIn-place actions on SharePoint/OneDrive files: unshare, delete, apply label",s:"Policy, sensitive types, user and file with in-place actions",
  x:"The alert story lists the user activity that triggered the match; the Sensitive info types tab shows which classifiers fired; the Source tab reveals the file content to users with the right permission. Remediation buttons let the analyst apply a retention or sensitivity label, unshare, delete or download the file, and download an offending email. A question about proving what sensitive data left and then revoking the share is answered on this page."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.2",q:"Hunting Purview DLP activity — which advanced hunting table",a:"#CloudAppEvents holds the SharePoint, OneDrive, Exchange and device audit records\nGo hunt offers built-in queries such as user DLP violations for the last 30 days",s:"CloudAppEvents, reachable through Go hunt on the alert",k:"CloudAppEvents\n| where ActionType has \"DLP\"\n| where AccountUpn =~ \"ana@fabrikam.com\"",
  x:"DLP evidence is audit data, so it lands in CloudAppEvents rather than a dedicated endpoint table. The Go hunt control on a DLP alert pre-builds queries like file shared with, file activities and site activity, scoped to the user or file. On the exam, if the stem asks you to find every file a flagged user shared externally in the last month, CloudAppEvents is the table to pick."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.2",q:"Insider risk severity on the Defender user page — where it appears",a:"#The identity Overview tab shows an Insider risk severity card when Purview IRM is enabled\nLets the SOC weigh Purview risk alongside alerts and UEBA anomalies",s:"Overview card on the identity page sourced from Purview IRM",
  x:"Insider Risk Management scores users on exfiltration and policy-driven indicators; with the integration on, that severity surfaces on the user's Overview tab in Defender next to Entra attributes, AD flags and recent alerts. It is context for prioritisation, not an alert in itself. Expect the exam to describe an analyst who wants Purview's insider signal without opening the Purview portal; the identity page is where it lives."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.2",q:"Compromised entity identified by Purview — the pivot in Defender",a:"#Select the user card on the DLP alert to open the identity and take identity actions\nDevice card opens the endpoint for device-side remediation",s:"User and device cards on the alert lead to response actions",
  x:"A Purview alert is only the clue; remediation happens on the entity. From the DLP alert page, the user card opens the Defender identity page where you can require sign-in again, suspend in Entra or mark the account compromised, and the device card opens the device page for isolation or scan. The exam likes the chain 'DLP flagged a bulk download, now stop the account': alert, user card, response."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.2",q:"Why Purview and XDR alerts correlate into one incident",a:"#Defender XDR groups alerts sharing users, devices or files, including DLP alerts\nA data exfiltration alert can join an endpoint or identity incident automatically",s:"Shared entities pull DLP alerts into cross-domain incidents",
  x:"Incident correlation in Defender XDR is entity-driven: when a DLP alert names the same user as a risky sign-in or a Defender for Endpoint detection, they roll up into one incident with a combined attack story. The benefit on the exam is phrased as 'reduce the number of separate investigations' or 'see the data-loss step within the broader attack'. Severity of the incident follows the highest alert inside it."},
 /* 2.1.3 Defender for Cloud */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.3",q:"Defender for Cloud alert severity — High",a:"#High probability the resource is compromised; investigate immediately\nExample: a known malicious tool such as Mimikatz executed on a VM",s:"Resource very likely compromised, act now",g:"dfcsev",
  x:"Defender for Cloud scores each alert by confidence in both the detection and the malicious intent. High means both are strong, so the exam expects you to triage these first on the Security alerts page. Compare with Medium (suspicious, possibly compromised) and Low (possibly benign or already blocked). A stem listing alert severities and asking which to handle first is this card."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.3",q:"Defender for Cloud alert severity — Medium",a:"#Suspicious activity that may indicate compromise\nExample: sign-in from an unusual location flagged by anomaly analytics",s:"Suspicious, possible compromise, moderate confidence",g:"dfcsev",
  x:"Medium combines moderate confidence in the analytic with medium-to-high confidence that the intent is malicious, which is typical of machine-learning and anomaly detections. These need investigation but after High alerts. On the exam, 'anomalous', 'unusual' and 'ML-based' in a Defender for Cloud alert description usually map to Medium rather than High."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.3",q:"Defender for Cloud alert severity — Low and Informational",a:"#Low: possibly benign or a blocked attack, low confidence of intent\nInformational: benign alone but meaningful inside a correlated incident",s:"Low is likely benign or blocked; Informational needs context",g:"dfcsev",
  x:"Log clearing is the classic Low example: an admin housekeeping task or an attacker hiding tracks. Informational alerts are dismissed in bulk by many teams but Defender for Cloud keeps them because a security incident (correlated alerts) can turn several Informational items into a real pattern. Exam tip: when building a Sentinel incident creation rule for Defender for Cloud alerts, filter out Informational to avoid noise."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.3",q:"Workload protection plans that raise Defender for Cloud alerts",a:"#Servers, Storage, Containers, SQL, Key Vault, DNS, Resource Manager, App Service, APIs\nNo plan enabled on the subscription means no alerts for that resource type",s:"Per-resource Defender plans are the alert sources",
  x:"Each Defender for Cloud plan watches a different plane: Servers covers VMs and on-premises machines, Storage covers blob and file activity, Resource Manager covers control-plane operations, Key Vault covers secret access, DNS covers resolution patterns. A stem saying 'alerts are missing for the SQL database' with a plan not enabled is asking you to turn on Defender for SQL, not to tune Sentinel."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.3",q:"MITRE ATT&CK on a Defender for Cloud alert — where it shows",a:"#Each alert carries a kill-chain intent mapped to ATT&CK tactics\nVisible on the alert details alongside affected resources and remediation steps",s:"Tactic mapping shown on every alert's details",
  x:"The alert page in Defender for Cloud lists severity, status, activity time, description, affected resource and the MITRE tactic (kill chain intent), plus detailed technical data such as IPs, files and processes in the full view. Alerts are retained in the portal for 90 days even if the resource is deleted and can be exported to CSV. Exam clue: 'identify which attack stage the alert represents' is the tactic field."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.3",q:"Take action tab on a Defender for Cloud alert — the five options",a:"#Inspect resource context, Mitigate the threat, Prevent future attacks, Trigger automated response, Suppress similar alerts\nAutomated response runs a Logic App",s:"Context, mitigate, prevent, Logic App, suppress",
  x:"The Take action tab is the response hub: it links to the resource's activity logs, lists manual mitigation steps for that alert type, points to recommendations that would prevent recurrence, lets you fire a workflow automation (Logic App) and lets you build a suppression rule. On the exam, 'run an automated remediation when this alert fires again' maps to Trigger automated response, not to a Sentinel playbook."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.3",q:"Suppression rules in Defender for Cloud — what they are for",a:"#Hide future alerts matching known-benign conditions, such as a sanctioned scanner\nCreated from Suppress similar alerts on an alert or from the alerts page",s:"Mute repeat alerts from an approved, benign source",
  x:"A suppression rule names the alert type and optional entity conditions (an IP, a process, a host) and an expiry, after which matching alerts are not shown. It reduces fatigue without changing the detection itself. Distinguish from Sentinel automation rules that close incidents, and from Defender XDR alert tuning rules for endpoint alerts. Stem clue: 'vulnerability scanner triggers the same Defender for Cloud alert nightly' means create a suppression rule."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.3",q:"Bi-directional alert sync between Defender for Cloud and Sentinel",a:"#Closing the Sentinel incident closes the originating Defender for Cloud alert\nDefault is one-way (Defender for Cloud to Sentinel); enable on the connector",s:"Incident closure flows back to Defender for Cloud if enabled",
  x:"The Defender for Cloud connector always pushes alert status into Sentinel, so an alert dismissed in Defender for Cloud appears closed in Sentinel. Turning on bi-directional sync adds the reverse direction and needs Contributor or Security Admin on the subscription. The tenant-based connector variant covers every subscription at once. Exam wording: 'analysts close incidents in Sentinel but alerts stay open in Defender for Cloud' is solved by enabling bi-directional sync.",
  w:"Default is one way down; flip the switch for two ways."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.3",q:"Just-in-time VM access — its place in response",a:"#Defender for Servers Plan 2 feature that opens management ports only on request\nDefault ports 22, 3389, 5985, 5986; default maximum window 3 hours",s:"On-demand opening of RDP/SSH ports for a time window",
  x:"JIT keeps NSG or Azure Firewall rules closed for management ports until a user requests access from a specific source IP for a limited time, then closes them again. During investigation the JIT activity log shows who opened which port and when, which is useful evidence for a brute-force alert on a VM. The exam frames it as reducing exposure after a Defender for Cloud alert about RDP brute force.",
  w:"JIT = management ports closed by default, opened on a timer."},
 /* 2.1.4 Defender for Cloud Apps */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"Activity policy in Defender for Cloud Apps",a:"#Watches specific user actions via the app connector API and alerts on patterns or volume\nExample: many failed sign-ins or mass downloads in one session",s:"API-driven rule on specific user activities",g:"dfcapol",
  x:"Activity policies use the connected app's API to inspect events after the fact and can fire on a single matching activity or a repeated-activity threshold. Governance actions include notify user, suspend user, require sign-in again and confirm user compromised. Contrast with session policies, which act in real time through the proxy. Exam clue: 'alert when a user downloads more than N files within M minutes' is an activity policy."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"Anomaly detection policy in Defender for Cloud Apps",a:"#Built-in machine learning that flags deviations from a learned baseline\nSeven-day learning period; examples include impossible travel and infrequent country",s:"Machine-learned deviations from the user baseline",g:"dfcapol",
  x:"Anomaly detection policies are pre-built and mostly tunable rather than authored: you adjust sensitivity and scope, not the logic. The engine needs about seven days of data to learn and compares against roughly thirty days of history across risk factors like location, device, activity rate and admin actions. Since mid-2025 many legacy detections migrated to dynamic names. On the exam, 'no rule was written but an alert fired' signals an anomaly policy."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"File policy in Defender for Cloud Apps",a:"#Scans connected cloud storage for sensitive content or file types and applies governance\nRetires January 6, 2027; Microsoft steers to Purview DLP",s:"Content-based scan of stored files with governance actions",g:"dfcapol",
  x:"File policies inspect files at rest in connected apps, matching on data types such as credit card numbers, sharing level or owner, then take actions like remove external sharing, apply a sensitivity label, user quarantine or admin quarantine. Because the capability is being retired in favour of Purview DLP, the exam may present a migration scenario. Stem clue: 'publicly shared file containing PII in SharePoint' is the classic file policy case."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"Session policy vs access policy in Defender for Cloud Apps",a:"#Access policy decides whether the sign-in proceeds; session policy controls actions inside\nBoth run through Conditional Access App Control",s:"Gate at sign-in versus controls during the session",g:"dfcapol",
  x:"Access policies block or allow entry based on device, location, client app or certificate, for example blocking native clients from unmanaged devices. Session policies watch the live browser session and can block download, cut, copy and print, label on download, block unlabeled uploads, block malware or require step-up authentication. On the exam, 'allow access but prevent downloading sensitive files from an unmanaged device' is a session policy.",
  w:"Access = door; session = what you may do inside."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"OAuth app policy and app discovery policy — what each detects",a:"#OAuth app policy: risky permissions granted to third-party apps\nApp discovery policy: new or risky apps found in Cloud Discovery traffic",s:"Consent risk versus shadow-IT discovery",g:"dfcapol",
  x:"OAuth app policies look at the permissions apps have been granted in the tenant and can revoke or ban an app; app discovery policies watch Cloud Discovery data and alert when a newly seen app exceeds a usage or risk threshold. Exam distinction: a question about an app that users consented to with mail.read permissions is OAuth, while a question about unknown SaaS traffic from the proxy logs is discovery."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"Governance actions on a user in Defender for Cloud Apps",a:"#Notify user, suspend user, require user to sign in again, confirm user compromised\nConfirm compromised sets Entra user risk to high so risk policies fire",s:"Suspend, re-authenticate or mark high-risk via Entra",
  x:"These actions ride on the Entra ID integration: require sign-in again revokes refresh tokens and session cookies, suspend removes access immediately, and confirm compromised pushes the user into high risk so a user-risk Conditional Access policy can force a secure password change. The exam often asks for the action that forces re-authentication without disabling the account, which is require user to sign in again.",
  w:"Confirm compromised hands the user to Entra risk policies."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"Cloud Discovery — where the data comes from",a:"#Defender for Endpoint integration, log collector, firewall/proxy logs, SWG partners\nSnapshot reports for ad-hoc uploads; continuous reports for streaming",s:"Endpoint sensor, log collector or uploaded proxy logs",
  x:"Cloud Discovery compares traffic logs against a catalog of more than 31,000 apps, each scored 1 to 10 across 90-plus risk factors. The Defender for Endpoint integration needs no extra appliance and covers off-network devices. Marking an app unsanctioned can push block indicators to endpoints via network protection. Exam clue: 'identify shadow IT used from laptops at home' means enable the Defender for Endpoint integration."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"Conditional Access App Control — how sessions get routed",a:"#An Entra Conditional Access policy sends the session through the Defender for Cloud Apps proxy\nNon-Edge browsers see the .mcas.ms suffix; Edge protects in-browser",s:"Entra policy routes browser sessions through the proxy",
  x:"The session control named Use Conditional Access App Control in Entra Conditional Access hands the browser session to Defender for Cloud Apps, where access and session policies apply in real time. It covers browser traffic only (SAML 2.0 or OpenID Connect apps), not desktop clients. On the exam, a user noticing a .mcas.ms address bar or a lock icon in Edge is the tell that app control is in play."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"Impossible travel vs activity from infrequent country",a:"#Impossible travel: two activities too far apart in too little time\nInfrequent country: location never or rarely seen for that user",s:"Speed-based anomaly versus never-seen location",
  x:"Both are anomaly detections, but they ask different questions. Impossible travel needs two events and compares distance against elapsed time, with VPN-aware suppression and a sensitivity slider. Infrequent country needs only one event and compares against the user's and tenant's history. The exam may add that a VPN egress point explains the alert; that is the impossible travel false-positive case, tuned by lowering sensitivity or adding the IP range.",
  w:"Two points too fast = impossible travel; one new map pin = infrequent country."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.4",q:"Investigation priority score on the user page",a:"#Single number ranking a user by alerts, anomalous activity and potential impact\nShown on the identity inventory as the risk score and on the user page",s:"Composite user ranking that says who to look at first",
  x:"Defender for Cloud Apps and Defender XDR fold a user's open alerts, abnormal behaviours and attributes such as privileged group membership into one score so analysts can sort the identity inventory by exposure rather than by alert count. The score drops as risk is dismissed or remediated. Exam wording like 'determine which of these users to investigate first' is answered by sorting on this score."},
 /* 2.1.5 Entra ID Protection */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"User risk vs sign-in risk in Entra ID Protection",a:"#User risk: probability the identity itself is compromised (credentials leaked)\nSign-in risk: probability this particular authentication is not the owner",s:"Account compromise versus one suspicious authentication",g:"idprisk",
  x:"Identity Protection keeps two separate scores. Detections such as leaked credentials or anomalous user activity raise user risk, which persists until remediated; detections such as unfamiliar sign-in properties or anonymous IP raise the risk of one sign-in. Policies map to each: user risk policy requires a secure password change, sign-in risk policy requires MFA. A stem about a password found in a dump is user risk; a stem about a login from a Tor exit node is sign-in risk.",
  w:"User risk = who; sign-in risk = this one login."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"Risk levels in Entra ID Protection",a:"#Low, Medium, High per detection; aggregated per user and per sign-in\nPolicies are usually set to act at Medium and above or High only",s:"Low, Medium and High, aggregated per user and per sign-in",g:"idprisk",
  x:"Each detection carries a level reflecting confidence and impact: leaked credentials and suspicious MFA approval are High, while noisier signals are Low. The risk shown on a user is the aggregate of their unremediated detections. Microsoft's guidance is a user risk policy at High and a sign-in risk policy at Medium and above, which is the pairing the exam expects when it asks for the least disruptive secure configuration."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"Real-time vs offline risk detections",a:"#Real-time: evaluated during authentication so Conditional Access can react instantly\nOffline: computed afterwards and raise risk later, e.g. leaked credentials, atypical travel",s:"Evaluated during sign-in versus calculated afterwards",
  x:"Real-time detections such as anonymous IP, unfamiliar sign-in properties, verified threat actor IP and password spray can be acted on by a sign-in risk policy before the token is issued. Offline detections like leaked credentials, atypical travel, malicious IP and anomalous token arrive later, so the user's risk increases after the session already exists. Exam clue: 'the user was already signed in when risk appeared' points to an offline detection and a need to revoke sessions.",
  w:"Real-time blocks the door; offline knocks after you are inside."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"Leaked credentials detection — what it means and its tier",a:"#Microsoft found the user's valid credentials in a public or dark-web dump\nUser-risk detection, offline, available without a P2 license",s:"Credentials seen in a breach dump; raises user risk",
  x:"Leaked credentials is one of the non-premium detections alongside anonymous IP, Entra threat intelligence and admin-confirmed compromise, so even a P1 tenant sees it. Because the password is known to be exposed, the correct response is a secure password change, either via a user risk policy or an admin reset. The exam contrasts it with premium detections such as password spray or anomalous token that need P2."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"Anomalous token vs unfamiliar sign-in properties",a:"#Anomalous token: token lifetime or replay from an unfamiliar place suggests theft\nUnfamiliar sign-in properties: device, location, IP or network the user has never used",s:"Token theft signal versus never-seen sign-in attributes",
  x:"Both are premium detections but they point at different attacks. Anomalous token (sign-in and user flavours) fires when a token is used in a way inconsistent with its issuance, the hallmark of adversary-in-the-middle or token replay, and MFA alone no longer remediates it: a secure password change plus re-authentication is needed. Unfamiliar sign-in properties is real-time and compares the current attributes against the user's history.",
  w:"Stolen token means reset and revoke, not another MFA prompt."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"Password spray and Entra threat intelligence detections",a:"#Password spray: one password tried across many accounts, flagged in real time or offline\nEntra threat intelligence: Microsoft's internal or external sources call the activity malicious",s:"One password, many accounts, versus Microsoft-known bad",
  x:"Password spray is a premium sign-in detection that exists for both real-time and offline evaluation. Microsoft Entra threat intelligence is non-premium and covers both sign-in and user flavours; it is the catch-all for patterns Microsoft's intelligence recognises as attack infrastructure. On the exam, a stem citing 'a known attack pattern reported by Microsoft' is threat intelligence, while 'low-and-slow attempts across the tenant' is spray."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"Confirm compromised vs dismiss on a risky user",a:"#Confirm compromised sets risk to High and records an admin-confirmed detection\nDismiss marks the risk a false positive and clears it; neither resets the password",s:"Escalate to confirmed-breached versus clear as benign",g:"idpaction",
  x:"These are the two triage verdicts on the Risky users and Risky sign-ins reports. Confirm compromised feeds the signal back to Microsoft and lets risk policies act; follow it with password reset, block, and revoke tokens. Dismiss user risk sets the state to Dismissed with the detail 'admin dismissed all risk for user'. The exam trap is assuming dismiss changes the password; it only changes the risk state.",
  w:"Confirm feeds the model; dismiss cleans the slate."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"Self-remediation through risk-based Conditional Access",a:"#User risk policy: require password change (secure change with MFA)\nSign-in risk policy: require MFA; passing it marks the sign-in Remediated",s:"Password change for user risk, MFA for sign-in risk",g:"idpaction",
  x:"Risk-based Conditional Access lets users clear their own risk without a help desk ticket. A medium-and-above sign-in risk policy prompts for MFA, and the risk detail becomes 'user passed multifactor authentication'. A high user risk policy forces a secure password change, which is MFA plus a new password rather than the SSPR flow. Hybrid users can remediate on-premises only if the 'allow on-premises password change to reset user risk' setting is enabled with password hash sync.",
  w:"MFA clears sign-in risk; a password change clears user risk."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"Admin manual remediation on a risky user — reset vs block vs revoke",a:"#Reset password issues a temporary password and sets risk to Remediated\nBlock sign-in stops access; Revoke sessions kills refresh tokens so existing sessions end",s:"Temporary password, sign-in block, or session revocation",g:"idpaction",
  x:"From Risky users an admin with Security Operator plus User Administrator can reset the password, which clears risk with the detail 'admin generated temporary password'. Blocking the user and revoking sessions are the containment steps when a password change alone would leave an attacker's existing tokens valid. Exam wording: 'the attacker still has access after the password was changed' is answered by revoking sessions."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.5",q:"Risky workload identities",a:"#Service principals and app registrations get their own risk detections and report\nRemediate by rotating credentials, disabling the principal or blocking with Conditional Access",s:"Risk scoring for service principals, not just users",
  x:"Identity Protection extends beyond users: workload identities get detections such as leaked credentials for an app or suspicious sign-in patterns, visible on the Risky workload identities report. Conditional Access for workload identities can block risky service principals, and the usual remediation is to rotate the secret or certificate. The exam signals this with 'an application's client secret was posted on GitHub' and asks where the risk appears."},
 /* 2.1.6 Defender for Identity */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"Where Defender for Identity sensors are installed",a:"#On every domain controller, plus AD FS, AD CS and Entra Connect servers\nOnly domain controller sensors execute remediation actions",s:"Domain controllers plus AD FS, AD CS and Entra Connect",
  x:"The sensor reads network traffic and events on the box it sits on, so each domain controller needs its own sensor for complete coverage. AD FS sensors watch federation sign-ins, AD CS sensors watch certificate issuance, and Entra Connect sensors watch sync-account abuse; they do not run response actions. Exam stem: 'alerts are missing for one site' usually means a DC in that site has no sensor."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"Defender for Identity alert categories",a:"#Reconnaissance and discovery; persistence and privilege escalation; credential access; lateral movement; other\nOrdered like the kill chain",s:"Recon, persistence, credential access, lateral movement",g:"dfialert",
  x:"Alert documentation groups detections by attack phase. Reconnaissance covers LDAP, DNS and SAMR enumeration; persistence and privilege escalation covers golden ticket variants, skeleton key and sensitive-group changes; credential access covers brute force, AS-REP roasting, DCSync and honeytoken authentication; lateral movement covers pass-the-hash, pass-the-ticket and overpass-the-hash. The exam may show an alert name and ask which phase it indicates."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"Pass-the-hash vs pass-the-ticket alerts",a:"#Pass-the-hash: a stolen NTLM hash reused from another device\nPass-the-ticket: a Kerberos TGT reused from a device other than where it was issued",s:"Reused NTLM hash versus reused Kerberos ticket",g:"dfialert",
  x:"Both are flagged as suspected identity theft in the lateral movement category. Defender for Identity spots pass-the-hash by seeing an NTLM authentication with a hash observed earlier on a different host, and pass-the-ticket by seeing the same ticket presented from two machines. Overpass-the-hash is the Kerberos cousin where a hash is used to request a ticket, often with a weaker encryption type. On the exam the protocol named in the stem decides the answer."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"Golden ticket alerts — the variants",a:"#Forged TGT signed with the krbtgt hash; flagged by encryption downgrade, nonexistent account, ticket or time anomaly\nPersistence and privilege escalation category",s:"Forged TGT detected through anomalies in the ticket",g:"dfialert",
  x:"Because a golden ticket is self-signed with the krbtgt key, Defender for Identity cannot see the forgery directly and instead catches side effects: a ticket for an account that does not exist, a lifetime longer than policy allows, an unexpected encryption type, or data that does not match the authorization fields. Remediation is a double reset of krbtgt. Exam clue: 'the account named in the ticket was deleted months ago' is the nonexistent-account variant.",
  w:"Golden ticket = reset krbtgt twice."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"DCSync vs DCShadow alerts",a:"#DCSync: a non-DC account requests directory replication to pull hashes\nDCShadow: a rogue machine registers as a DC to push changes",s:"Impostor pulls replication versus fake DC pushes changes",g:"dfialert",
  x:"DCSync abuses the replication rights (DS-Replication-Get-Changes) so an attacker can read every password hash including krbtgt; it is a credential access alert. DCShadow is the write direction: the attacker promotes a fake domain controller and replicates malicious changes in, which lands in the other category with two sub-alerts (promotion and replication request). The exam distinguishes by direction of data flow: read is DCSync, write is DCShadow.",
  w:"Sync pulls secrets out; Shadow pushes changes in."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"AS-REP roasting vs Kerberoasting",a:"#AS-REP roasting: accounts without pre-authentication leak crackable AS-REP data\nKerberoasting: service tickets for SPN accounts are requested and cracked offline",s:"No-preauth account abuse versus SPN ticket cracking",g:"dfialert",
  x:"Both are credential access alerts (Suspected AS-REP Roasting attack and Suspected Kerberos SPN exposure). AS-REP roasting needs a user with 'do not require Kerberos preauthentication' set, so the fix is to enable pre-authentication; Kerberoasting targets accounts with a servicePrincipalName and a weak password, so the fix is long passwords or gMSAs for services. Exam clue: 'pre-authentication disabled' decides it is AS-REP.",
  w:"AS-REP = preauth off; Kerberoast = SPN with weak password."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"Honeytoken alerts",a:"#A decoy account you tag; any authentication, LDAP query or attribute change raises an alert\nNear-zero false positives because nobody should touch it",s:"Decoy account that alerts on any use",
  x:"Honeytoken accounts are configured under Defender for Identity settings and generate alerts such as Honeytoken authentication activity, Honeytoken was queried via LDAP and Honeytoken user attributes modified. Because the account has no legitimate use, any activity is high-signal evidence of reconnaissance or credential theft. On the exam, a request to detect attackers enumerating accounts with minimal false positives is this feature.",
  w:"Honeytoken: bait that should never bite."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"Brute force and NTLM relay alerts",a:"#Suspected Brute Force attack (Kerberos, NTLM) and a separate SMB brute force alert\nSuspected NTLM relay attack (Exchange account) flags coerced authentication relayed to another host",s:"Password guessing versus relayed-authentication detection",g:"dfialert",
  x:"Brute force shows up as many failed authentications followed by a success, which Defender for Identity reports in the credential access or lateral movement categories depending on protocol. NTLM relay catches an Exchange server's machine account authentication being replayed to another service, a classic path to domain dominance. Remediation differs: lockout and MFA for brute force, SMB and LDAP signing or EPA for relay."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"Response actions on an on-premises AD user from Defender",a:"#Disable user in AD, enable user in AD, force password reset\nTaken from the identity page, side panel, advanced hunting or by attack disruption",s:"Disable, enable or force a reset through the DC sensor",
  x:"The Defender portal can act on Active Directory because a domain controller sensor executes the change using its local system account or a configured gMSA, set under Settings, Identities, Manage action accounts. Disable and enable need Domain Admin-equivalent rights on the operator; force password change can be done by Authentication, User, Password or Helpdesk Administrators. Exam stem: 'disable the on-prem account without RDP to a DC' is this feature."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.6",q:"Attack paths tab on the identity page",a:"#Shows potential lateral movement routes from this identity toward sensitive accounts\nComplements Security recommendations from identity posture assessments",s:"Lateral-movement routes toward sensitive accounts",
  x:"The identity page in Defender gathers Overview, Incidents and alerts, Observed in organization, Risk score, Timeline, Security recommendations, Attack paths, Policies and Sentinel events. Attack paths visualises how an attacker who owns this account could hop to privileged accounts through shared sessions or local admin rights, which is the lateral movement path concept. Exam clue: 'reduce the exposure of domain admins to a compromised helpdesk account' means review this tab and remove the admin rights it shows."},
 /* 2.1.7 Sentinel incidents */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Sentinel incident status values",a:"#New, Active, Closed\nClosing requires a classification",s:"New, Active and Closed",
  x:"Every Sentinel incident starts as New, moves to Active when someone owns the investigation, and ends Closed with a classification and optional comment. Automation rules can change status, for example setting Active when assigning an owner. In the Defender portal the equivalent states read Active, In progress and Resolved, so read the stem for which portal it is describing. A question about reopening is simply setting the status back from Closed."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Closing classification — True positive",a:"#Suspicious activity: the alert reflected a real attack\nThe other three are Benign positive, False positive and Undetermined",s:"Real attack confirmed",g:"incclass",
  x:"When closing a Sentinel incident you pick one of four classifications and may add a reason. True positive with suspicious activity means the detection was right and the activity was hostile. Choose it after remediation so the analytics rule's precision metrics and SOC optimization recommendations reflect reality. Exam clue: 'the user's account was confirmed compromised' closes as True positive.",
  w:"True positive = right detection, bad activity."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Closing classification — Benign positive",a:"#Suspicious but expected: the rule fired correctly on authorised activity\nExample: a scheduled penetration test or sanctioned admin script",s:"Right detection, legitimate activity",g:"incclass",
  x:"Benign positive is the subtle one: the logic was correct, the behaviour looked like an attack, but it was approved. Red-team exercises, vulnerability scans and change-window scripts are typical. Picking Benign positive rather than False positive matters because it tells tuning efforts that the rule is fine and only an exception or allow-list is needed. Exam wording like 'the activity was performed by the security team during testing' is Benign positive.",
  w:"Benign positive: the rule is right, the actor is friendly."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Closing classification — False positive vs Undetermined",a:"#False positive: inaccurate detection, nothing suspicious happened\nUndetermined: not enough information to decide",s:"Wrong detection, or not enough evidence to say",g:"incclass",
  x:"False positive means the analytics logic misfired on normal activity, so the follow-up is to tune the rule or add an automation rule that auto-closes the pattern. Undetermined is the honest fallback when logs were missing or the window expired; it keeps metrics clean without claiming a verdict. On the exam, 'the rule matched a legitimate service account doing its job and must be tuned' is False positive, while 'logs for that period were not retained' is Undetermined."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Sentinel incident severity values",a:"#Informational, Low, Medium, High\nSet by the analytics rule; changeable manually or by automation rule",s:"Informational, Low, Medium, High",
  x:"Severity in Sentinel is a property of the rule that created the incident, so two incidents from the same rule start at the same level. Analysts raise or lower it on the incident; automation rules can change it on creation based on conditions such as a watchlist of VIP users. The Defender portal uses the same four levels for Sentinel incidents once the workspace is onboarded. Distinguish from classification, which only exists at closure."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Sentinel incident page — what the Overview tab holds",a:"#Incident timeline of alerts and bookmarks, Similar incidents, Entities, Top insights\nLeft pane: evidence counts, first entities, owner, Investigate button",s:"Timeline, similar incidents, entities and insights widgets",
  x:"The Overview tab is the triage dashboard: the timeline lists alerts and hunting bookmarks in order, the Entities widget lets you add an entity to threat intelligence or run a playbook on it, and Top insights runs researcher-written queries over a window from 24 hours before the first alert. The Entities tab gives each entity an info card, a seven-day timeline and insights. A stem asking where to see UEBA insights for the incident's user is this page."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Similar incidents in Sentinel — how similarity is judged",a:"#Shared entities, the same analytics rule, or matching incident details\nUp to 20 are listed with a similarity reason column",s:"Same entities, same rule or same details, up to 20 shown",
  x:"The widget helps an analyst see whether the current incident is part of a wave or a repeat false positive. Each row explains why it matched, and hovering shows the common items. It does not merge incidents; merging in Sentinel happens through the Defender portal's correlation once onboarded, or manually by adding alerts. Exam clue: 'identify previous incidents involving the same IP address' is Similar incidents, not the investigation graph."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Investigation graph in Sentinel — requirements and use",a:"#Visual map of alerts and mapped entities, with exploration queries per node\nNeeds entity mapping on the rule; incidents up to 30 days old",s:"Entity graph with exploration queries; needs entity mapping",
  x:"Selecting Investigate opens the graph, where each entity node offers researcher-built exploration queries, related alerts appear with dotted lines, and a timeline shows the sequence. If a custom rule has no entity mapping the graph is empty, which is the trap the exam sets: the fix is to add entity mappings to the analytics rule, not to change the incident. Raw events are reachable through the Events link into Log Analytics."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Incident tasks in Sentinel — three ways to add them",a:"#Manually on the incident, by an automation rule Add task action, or by a playbook\nAnalysts tick them off in the Tasks panel; Responder role suffices",s:"Manual, automation rule, or playbook-added checklist items",
  x:"Tasks turn tribal knowledge into a checklist on each incident. Automation rules are the recommended path for static steps and can be scoped by analytics rule name; playbooks use the Sentinel connector's Add task to incident and Mark task as completed actions when a task should be created conditionally or auto-closed after an API call succeeds. The exam asks for the simplest way to put the same five steps on every incident from one rule: automation rule."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.7",q:"Sentinel incidents in the unified Defender portal",a:"#Onboarding the workspace merges Sentinel and XDR incidents into one queue with bi-directional sync\nThe Microsoft Defender XDR connector is configured automatically",s:"One incident queue, synced both ways, once onboarded",
  x:"Once a workspace (one primary, optional secondaries) is connected to the Defender portal, incident changes made in either portal replicate to the other, advanced hunting spans both data sets, and features such as analytics rules, automation rules, workbooks and watchlists appear in the Defender navigation. Offboarding disconnects the XDR connector. Exam clue: 'analysts want a single queue for Sentinel and Defender incidents' is onboarding, not a playbook."},
 /* 2.1.8 Copilot and agentic AI */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.8",q:"Embedded Security Copilot in Defender — the incident capabilities",a:"#Incident summary, guided response, incident report, device and identity summaries\nContext-aware: it already knows which incident is open",s:"Summaries, guided next steps and reports inside the incident",
  x:"The Copilot pane in the Defender portal reads the current incident and offers a plain-language summary, recommended response steps with one-click actions, and a post-incident report. Device and identity summaries pull the same context for assets. It uses the preinstalled Defender, Threat Intelligence and natural-language-to-KQL plugins. Exam clue: 'reduce the time a tier-1 analyst needs to understand an incident' is the embedded summary."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.8",q:"Script analysis and file analysis with Copilot in Defender",a:"#Explains an obfuscated PowerShell or command line from the alert in plain language\nFile analysis summarises what a file does and whether it looks malicious",s:"Deobfuscation and intent explanation for scripts and files",
  x:"On an alert whose evidence includes a script or command line, the Analyze button sends it to Copilot, which returns a description of intent, indicators and suspicious techniques. File analysis works from the file page. Both are assistive: the analyst still decides and the verdict is not written back automatically. The exam contrasts this with deep analysis, which detonates a PE file in a sandbox rather than reasoning about its text."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.8",q:"Natural language to KQL in advanced hunting",a:"#Describe the hunt in English; Copilot drafts the query, you review and run it\nAvailable in the advanced hunting page, not only the standalone portal",s:"English prompt becomes an editable KQL query",
  x:"The NL2KQL plugin lets an analyst type something like 'show devices that contacted this IP in the last day' and receive a query against the right tables, which can be edited before running. It accelerates junior analysts but the exam expects you to know the output must still be validated; Copilot does not execute queries on its own. Stem wording 'analysts unfamiliar with KQL need to hunt' points here."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.8",q:"Embedded vs standalone Security Copilot",a:"#Embedded: lives inside Defender pages with page context\nStandalone: securitycopilot.microsoft.com with promptbooks, plugins and session history",s:"In-portal context helper versus the full chat workbench",
  x:"The standalone portal is where you run promptbooks (saved multi-step prompt sequences such as incident investigation), manage plugins and review sessions; embedded experiences surface the same engine inside Defender, Sentinel, Entra and Intune pages. Both consume the same Security Compute Units. Exam clue: 'run a repeatable sequence of prompts across many incidents' is a promptbook in the standalone portal."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.8",q:"Security Compute Units (SCUs) — the Copilot capacity model",a:"#Provisioned units per hour plus optional overage; exhausting both blocks prompts until the next hour\nUsage monitoring dashboard shows consumption by user, plugin and experience",s:"Hourly capacity units that every prompt and agent consumes",
  x:"An Azure capacity resource holds the provisioned SCU count; a Copilot owner who is also the Azure capacity owner or contributor can change it, with effect within about 30 minutes. The usage dashboard keeps 90 days of history and distinguishes prompt, promptbook and agent categories and manual versus automated actions. On the exam, 'analysts receive capacity errors mid-shift' is solved by raising provisioned or overage SCUs."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.8",q:"Phishing Triage Agent — what it does and does not do",a:"#Autonomously classifies user-reported phishing alerts as true or false positive with an explanation\nDoes not delete, quarantine or remediate; it classifies, tags and resolves alerts",s:"Classifies reported phish with reasoning; never remediates",
  x:"The agent triggers on the alert 'Email reported by user as malware or phish', analyses content, detonates URLs and files, and writes its verdict with a visual reasoning trail. False positives are resolved automatically; true positives stay open tagged Agent. It needs Defender for Office 365 Plan 2, unified RBAC, provisioned SCUs and an Agent ID identity, and the auto-resolve tuning rule for that alert must be off. Analysts can teach it through feedback, once per alert.",
  w:"The agent decides what it is; humans decide what to do."},
 /* 2.1.9 complex attacks */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.9",q:"Attack story tab on a Defender incident",a:"#Combines the alert list with the incident graph showing scope, chronology and entity links\nPlay the graph over time; open any node to act on it",s:"Alerts plus graph showing how the attack spread",
  x:"The attack story is the first stop for a multi-stage incident: the left side lists alerts in order and the graph on the right draws users, devices, mailboxes and files with the edges that connect them. Go hunt from a node runs entity-scoped queries such as all activity or related alerts. Exam clue: 'understand the sequence from the phishing email to the lateral movement on the server' is this tab, not the Alerts tab."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.9",q:"How a multi-domain incident gets its severity and its alerts",a:"#Severity equals the highest-severity alert inside it\nAlerts from email, identity, endpoint, cloud apps and Sentinel are correlated by shared entities",s:"Highest alert sets severity; shared entities group alerts",
  x:"Defender XDR builds incidents by linking alerts that share users, devices, files, IPs or mailboxes, regardless of which product raised them, so a single incident can carry Defender for Office 365, Defender for Identity and Defender for Endpoint alerts. Severity rolls up from the top alert but can be changed manually. When incidents merge, Defender may rename the incident, overwriting a custom name. Exam trap: lowering one alert's severity does not lower the incident unless it was the highest."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.9",q:"Assets and Evidence and Response tabs on a complex incident",a:"#Assets: devices, users, mailboxes, apps and cloud resources touched\nEvidence and Response: every entity with a verdict (Malicious, Suspicious, Clean) and remediation status",s:"Impacted assets versus per-entity verdicts and remediation",
  x:"Assets is the impact list, with inline actions such as initiate automated investigation on a device or open the identity page. Evidence and Response lists what the automated investigation judged and whether remediation is Pending approval, letting you approve or reject, open in Explorer or go hunt. On the exam, 'approve the pending soft delete for the malicious email entity' lives on Evidence and Response, while 'which servers were affected' is Assets."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.9",q:"Tracing lateral movement across devices and users in an incident",a:"#Pivot from a device node to its timeline, then from a user node to the identity page and attack paths\nInvestigate the SMB, RDP and remote-service events in the device timeline",s:"Device timeline to identity page pivots along the chain",
  x:"A lateral-movement investigation alternates between the two entity types: the device page timeline shows the logon events and processes that moved the attacker, while the user page shows which other devices that account touched and its risk score. Defender for Identity alerts such as pass-the-ticket mark the hop. The exam expects the workflow rather than one button: graph, device timeline, identity page, then contain the user to stop further hops."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.9",q:"Deception in Defender for Endpoint — decoy alerts in an incident",a:"#Lures (fake accounts, hosts, files) planted on devices; any interaction raises a high-fidelity alert\nDecoy activity in the attack story confirms an attacker is hands-on",s:"Planted lures whose use signals an active intruder",
  x:"Deception rules in Defender for Endpoint distribute decoy credentials and hosts; when an attacker reads the planted credential and tries to use it, the resulting alert has almost no benign explanation and lands in the incident alongside other evidence. During a complex investigation, a deception alert tells the analyst where the attacker currently is. Exam clue: 'detect lateral movement with minimal false positives' and 'lure' point to deception.",
  w:"If someone used the fake credentials, it was not an admin."},
 /* 2.1.10 case management */
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.10",q:"Case vs incident in the Defender portal",a:"#Incident: correlated alert activity created automatically\nCase: a SecOps work item holding tasks, evidence, comments, attachments and linked incidents",s:"Automatic correlation object versus a managed work item",
  x:"Cases live under the Cases page in the unified Defender portal and come in two kinds: incident cases created from an incident and generic cases created manually for work such as a threat hunt or a compliance request. A case tracks status, priority, assignee, tags, description, SLA policy and custom fields from a template. The exam frames it as 'track a long-running investigation spanning several incidents with ownership and due dates'."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.10",q:"Case tasks vs incident tasks",a:"#Case tasks carry owner, due date, priority, status and closing notes inside the case\nSentinel incident tasks are a checklist on one incident, often added by automation rules",s:"Full work items on a case versus a checklist on one incident",
  x:"Both are to-do lists but at different scopes. Incident tasks are tied to a single Sentinel incident and are usually templated by automation rules or playbooks. Case tasks are richer, with assignment and due dates, and sit on a case that may link several incidents. Exam clue: 'assign the forensic image collection to a named analyst with a deadline and track it across the whole engagement' is a case task."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.10",q:"Who can create and manage cases",a:"#Create and manage: Security operations / Alerts (manage) or Sentinel Responder\nView only: Security data basics (read) or Sentinel Reader; templates and SLA need Core security settings (manage)",s:"Alerts (manage) or Responder to create; Reader to view",
  x:"Permissions follow the unified RBAC model or the Sentinel Azure roles depending on tenant setup. Incident cases specifically use Security data read to view and Security data manage to edit. Configuring case templates, custom fields and SLA policies is an admin task requiring Core security settings (manage) or Sentinel Contributor. The exam will give you a role and ask whether the analyst can open, edit or configure cases."},
 {c:"xdrresp",obj:"2.1.10",q:"Prerequisite for case management",a:"#The tenant must be onboarded to the Defender portal; cases do not exist in the Azure portal Sentinel experience\nA connected Sentinel workspace is needed only for workspace-dependent scenarios",s:"Defender portal only, not Azure portal Sentinel",
  x:"Case management is a unified security operations feature, so a SOC still working Sentinel purely from the Azure portal cannot use it. Once the tenant is in the Defender portal, cases work with or without a Sentinel workspace, and the workspace is only required when a scenario depends on Sentinel data. Exam stem: 'the Cases page is missing' is a portal-onboarding question, not a licensing one."},
 /* ---------- dferesp (38) ---------- */
 /* 2.2.1 device timeline */
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.1",q:"Device timeline — what it shows",a:"#Chronological events and alerts for one device: processes, network, files, registry, logons\nDefault window is the past 30 days; events retained per the tenant's policy",s:"Per-device chronological event and alert stream",
  x:"The Timeline tab on the device page is the raw-evidence view behind every endpoint alert. Each row is an event with its process, user and MITRE technique where applicable, and selecting a row opens a side pane with the process tree and related entities. Exam clue: 'determine what happened on the device in the minutes before the alert' is the timeline, not the alert page."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.1",q:"Device timeline filters — Data type and Event group",a:"#Data type toggles Events versus Techniques; Event group narrows by category\nFlagged events filter shows only rows you marked",s:"Events or Techniques, by category, and flagged-only",
  x:"The filter pane reduces a 30-day stream to the slices that matter: Techniques shows only rows tagged with ATT&CK IDs, Event group selects categories such as process, network or registry, and the time bar narrows the window. Combined with the search box this is how you find a specific file name or command line. On the exam, 'show only the MITRE techniques observed on the host' means set Data type to Techniques."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.1",q:"Flag an event in the device timeline",a:"#Mark important rows with the flag column, then filter to Flagged events\nBuilds a clean breach timeline for the report",s:"Bookmark rows on the timeline for a clean narrative",
  x:"Flagging is per-event and per-device; it does not create a Sentinel bookmark or change the event. Analysts flag the initial access, persistence and lateral movement rows as they find them, then enable the Flagged events filter to review the attack as a sequence or hand it to a colleague. The exam phrases it as 'highlight key events for a handover without exporting everything'."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.1",q:"Hunt for related events from the timeline",a:"#Side-pane button that generates an advanced hunting query around the selected event\nReturns the event and what happened nearby on that device",s:"One-click KQL pivot from a timeline row",
  x:"Rather than hand-writing a query, selecting an event or technique and choosing Hunt for related events opens advanced hunting with a prebuilt KQL covering the same device and a time window around the event. From a Technique pane the query returns surrounding events but excludes the technique row itself. Exam clue: 'quickly pivot from a timeline entry to a query you can extend' is this button, not Go hunt on the incident graph."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.1",q:"Export the device timeline",a:"#Export to CSV for a chosen date or range, up to seven days at a time\nUsed for offline analysis or evidence preservation",s:"CSV export limited to a seven-day span",
  x:"The export button on the timeline toolbar writes the visible events with their full details to CSV. Because the span per export is capped at seven days, a 30-day investigation needs several exports or an advanced hunting query instead. Events are retained per the tenant's data retention setting (180 days by default) unless a connected Sentinel workspace extends it. On the exam, 'preserve device events beyond the retention period' means export or stream to a workspace."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.1",q:"Process tree in the timeline side pane",a:"#Parent-child chain for the selected event, with copyable hashes and command lines\nRelated entities drawn as a small graph",s:"Parent-child chain with copy buttons for artefacts",
  x:"Selecting a process event opens a pane showing who spawned what, the command line, SHA1 and the user context, with a copy icon for each artefact so you can pivot to the file page or a hunting query. The same tree appears on the alert page as the alert story. Exam clue: 'identify the parent process that launched the malicious script' is answered from this pane."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.1",q:"Device page tabs beyond the timeline",a:"#Overview, Alerts, Timeline, Security recommendations, Software inventory, Discovered vulnerabilities, Missing KBs\nOverview holds tags, risk level, exposure level and logged-on users",s:"Overview, alerts, posture tabs and inventories on one page",
  x:"The device page is the pivot hub for endpoint response: the Overview card shows domain, OS, health, risk and exposure, the Alerts tab lists detections, and the posture tabs show recommendations, installed software, CVEs and missing updates from Defender Vulnerability Management. Response actions sit on the top bar. On the exam, 'check whether the exploited CVE is present on the host' is the Discovered vulnerabilities tab."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.1",q:"MITRE Technique column in the timeline",a:"#Rows tagged with ATT&CK technique ID and name in bold with a blue icon\nSide pane links to tactics, description and the MITRE page",s:"ATT&CK tags inline with the events",
  x:"Techniques are derived rows: Defender for Endpoint infers them from telemetry even when no alert fired, so the timeline can show T1053 scheduled task creation for a benign script. They are searchable and exportable like events. The exam uses this to test that technique rows are context, not detections; a technique on the timeline does not mean an alert exists."},
 /* 2.2.2 actions on the device */
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Full isolation vs selective isolation",a:"#Full: cuts all network traffic except the Defender for Endpoint connection\nSelective: keeps Outlook, Teams and admin-defined exclusions reachable",s:"Everything cut versus exclusions kept reachable",g:"isotype",
  x:"Isolate device is the fastest containment: the sensor stays connected so you can still run live response and release later. Selective isolation lets the user keep working in Outlook and Teams and honours process and destination exclusions, which is the usual choice for an executive laptop during a low-confidence alert. Isolation lifts automatically after seven days. Exam clue: 'contain without stopping the user's email' is selective.",
  w:"Full isolation = only Defender talks; selective = plus Outlook/Teams."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Release from isolation and isolation retries",a:"#Release from isolation button on the device page; also undoable from the Action center\nIf the device is offline, the isolation request is retried for up to three days",s:"Manual release; offline devices are retried for three days",g:"isotype",
  x:"Isolation is one of the two actions the Action center explicitly lists as undoable, together with restrict code execution. If the device was off when you clicked Isolate, the sensor applies it when it next checks in within the retry window, and the Action center shows the pending state. On the exam, 'the user reports they can work again after a week' is the automatic seven-day lift, not a failed action."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Restrict app execution — the mechanism",a:"#Applies a Windows Defender Application Control policy allowing only Microsoft-signed binaries\nReversible with Remove app restrictions; needs Defender Antivirus active",s:"WDAC policy that blocks non-Microsoft-signed code",
  x:"Rather than cutting the network, restrict app execution stops the attacker's tooling from running by enforcing a code-integrity policy on the device. The user is notified on Windows 10 and 11 (not on older servers) and the device remains fully connected, so it suits cases where the host must stay online. Exam clue: 'prevent the malware from executing while keeping the server reachable' is restrict app execution, not isolation."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Run antivirus scan from the device page",a:"#Quick or Full scan launched remotely; results arrive as alerts and in the Action center\nWorks even when Defender Antivirus is in passive mode alongside another AV",s:"Remote quick or full scan, logged to the Action center",
  x:"The scan action is the lightest-touch response and is included in Plan 1. Quick scan checks common malware locations; Full scan walks the whole disk and is throttled by the average CPU load factor (default 50 percent). Detections found create new alerts and the action appears in the device timeline. On the exam it is the answer when the question says the SOC wants to confirm a device is clean without disrupting the user."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Collect investigation package — what is inside (Windows)",a:"#Autoruns, Installed programs, Network connections, Prefetch files, Processes, Scheduled tasks, Security event log\nSMB sessions, System information, Temp directories, Users and groups, WdSupportLogs, CollectionSummaryReport",s:"Autoruns, network, prefetch, processes, tasks, logs, summary",
  x:"The package is a zip of forensic snapshots gathered by the sensor, downloadable from the device page or the Action center entry 'Package collection available'. Network connections holds ActiveNetConnections, Arp, DnsCache, IpConfig and firewall logs; the security event log opens in Event Viewer; CollectionSummaryReport lists what succeeded. Collection can fail on low battery or a metered connection. Exam clue: 'retrieve persistence artefacts without a live session' is this action.",
  w:"Package = snapshot to download; live response = interactive shell."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Live response — session limits",a:"#Up to 50 concurrent sessions per tenant and 5 per user; one device per session\nSession times out after 30 minutes of inactivity",s:"Fifty tenant-wide, five per user, thirty-minute idle timeout",
  x:"Live response gives an interactive shell over the Defender for Endpoint channel, enabled in Settings, Endpoints, Advanced features, with a separate toggle for servers. Individual commands time out after 10 minutes, extended to 30 for getfile, findfile and run. The numbers appear on the exam as distractors, so remember 50 and 5. Unsigned PowerShell scripts need the dedicated unsigned script execution setting.",
  w:"50 sessions, 5 per analyst, 30 idle minutes."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Live response basic commands — getfile, findfile, fileinfo",a:"#getfile downloads a file (up to 3 GB); findfile locates by name; fileinfo returns metadata\nAll three work on Windows, macOS and Linux",s:"Download, locate and describe files on the device",g:"lrcmd",
  x:"These are the evidence-gathering trio. getfile pulls the suspicious binary into the portal for analysis, findfile searches the disk for a name pattern, and fileinfo returns hashes, size and timestamps without transferring the file. The exam often orders these in a drag-and-drop with putfile and remediate. Note that getfile may be followed by Submit for deep analysis from the file page."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Live response — processes, connections, registry, persistence",a:"#processes lists running processes; connections lists active sockets; registry reads values; persistence lists autostart points\nconnections, registry and persistence are Windows-only",s:"Enumerate processes, sockets, registry and autostarts",g:"lrcmd",
  x:"Triage commands show the live state that an investigation package only snapshots. processes is cross-platform; connections, drivers, registry, scheduledtasks, services, startupfolders and persistence exist only on Windows. A stem that mentions a macOS device and asks which command is unavailable is testing this platform matrix. Pair these with analyze on a suspicious entity to get an incrimination verdict."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Live response advanced commands — run, putfile, library",a:"#library lists scripts uploaded to the tenant library; putfile copies one to the device; run executes it\nUnsigned PowerShell needs the unsigned script execution setting",s:"Upload a script to the library, push it, execute it",g:"lrcmd",
  x:"Scripts are not typed into the session; they are uploaded once to the live response library (250 MB limit, 5 MB in US Government clouds), then referenced by run or putfile from any session. putfile is limited to 300 MB on Windows. The exam's ordering question usually goes: enable live response, upload script to library, connect, run script, review output."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Live response — remediate and undo",a:"#remediate removes an entity: delete file, stop process or service, delete registry entry\nundo restores the remediated entity (Windows only)",s:"Destructive cleanup with a Windows-only rollback",g:"lrcmd",
  x:"remediate is the in-session equivalent of an automated investigation action, applied to a file path, process ID, service or registry key you have already examined with analyze or fileinfo. undo reverses it if you were wrong. Every command runs under the device's system context and is logged to the Action center History tab. Exam clue: 'remove the persistence key immediately from the shell' is remediate."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Live response — isolate, release, scan, collect (macOS and Linux)",a:"#On macOS and Linux the session offers isolate, release, scan and collect\nOn Windows those are done from the device page instead",s:"Containment and collection from inside a non-Windows session",g:"lrcmd",
  x:"The command set differs by platform: Windows sessions have analyze, remediate and undo, while macOS and Linux sessions expose isolate and release (macOS), scan and collect so a single session can contain and gather forensics. The exam uses the matrix for 'which command is NOT available on this OS' items. Remember collect on macOS or Linux produces a forensics package similar to the Windows investigation package."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Initiate automated investigation from the device page",a:"#Starts an AIR investigation scoped to the device and its recent alerts\nVerdicts and pending remediations appear in the Investigations and Action center",s:"Kick off AIR for the device manually",
  x:"Automated investigation normally starts from an alert, but the device page button lets an analyst request one on a device with suspicious telemetry and no alert, for example after a user report. The investigation evaluates files and processes and queues remediations according to the device group's automation level. Note Microsoft announced that AIR for Defender for Endpoint stops running as a separate experience from September 2026; the Office 365 AIR remains."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Consult a threat expert and manage tags from the device page",a:"#Consult a threat expert opens an Endpoint Attack Notifications or Experts on Demand request with device context\nManage tags adds labels used for device groups and filtering",s:"Expert escalation and tag management on the device",
  x:"Both actions sit in the response bar but are not containment. Consult a threat expert requires the Defender Experts service to be set up; the request carries the device and alert context. Manage tags sets the device tag that drives device-group membership and therefore automation level and RBAC scope, so tagging a compromised host 'IR-hold' can move it into a group with full automation. Exam clue: 'change which automation level applies to a device' is a tag plus group."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.2",q:"Action center — Pending vs History",a:"#Pending: remediations waiting for approval; History: everything executed, manual or automated\nSupports approve, reject and undo for isolate and restrict code execution",s:"Approval queue versus audit log of executed actions",
  x:"The unified Action center under Actions and submissions spans Defender for Endpoint and Defender for Office 365. Pending holds AIR recommendations and proposed email remediations, grouped so you can approve several of one type at once; History records live response commands, scans, isolation and email soft deletes with who did them and when, exportable to CSV. Exam clue: 'find out who isolated the server last night' is History."},
 /* 2.2.3 evidence and entities */
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.3",q:"File page — prevalence and the Observed in organization section",a:"#Shows how many devices saw the file in the last six months and worldwide prevalence\nLists devices with first and last seen, execution status and initiating process",s:"Organisational and global prevalence of a hash",
  x:"Prevalence is the fastest way to size an incident: a file seen on three devices is contained differently from one seen on three thousand. Values are scoped by RBAC, so analysts with narrower device-group visibility see smaller numbers. The page also shows VirusTotal ratio, incidents and alerts, file names and, for PE files, strings, imports and capabilities mapped to ATT&CK. Exam clue: 'determine how widespread the malware is' is this page."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.3",q:"Deep analysis on the file page",a:"#Detonates a PE file in a Microsoft sandbox and reports behaviour in about ten minutes\nSubmit for deep analysis; results show process, network, file and registry activity",s:"Sandbox detonation report for a PE file",
  x:"Deep analysis is the behavioural counterpart to the static file page: Microsoft runs the file in an isolated environment and lists what it did, including dropped files and contacted hosts. It needs a copy of the file, so the file must have been collected from a device first (Collect file or getfile). The exam contrasts it with Copilot file analysis, which reasons about the file without detonation."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.3",q:"Stop and quarantine file vs add indicator",a:"#Stop and quarantine: kills the process and quarantines the file on devices now\nAdd indicator: hash-based block or allow applied going forward across the org",s:"Quarantine now versus a forward-looking block or allow rule",
  x:"Both live on the file page. Stop and quarantine is a one-time response on devices where the file is currently running or present; the indicator is a policy that prevents future execution (or exempts it) and can be scoped to device groups with an expiry. For a confirmed-malicious hash you usually do both. Exam clue: 'ensure the file cannot run on any device in future' is the indicator, not quarantine."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.3",q:"User page from an endpoint alert",a:"#Overview with risk score, Entra and AD details, devices signed into, alerts grouped by severity\nTimeline tab plus actions such as require sign-in again or disable in AD",s:"Identity hub reached from the alert's user entity",
  x:"When an alert names a user, selecting the entity opens the identity page that merges Defender for Identity, Entra ID and cloud-app context. Analysts check which other devices the account touched and its risk score before deciding whether the alert is a device problem or an account problem. Exam clue: 'the same user triggered alerts on several devices' means pivot to the user page and consider account containment."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.3",q:"IP, URL and domain entity pages",a:"#Show prevalence, alerts, devices that communicated with the entity and threat intelligence\nAdd indicator lets you block the IP, URL or domain across devices",s:"Network entity pivots with block indicators",
  x:"Opening an IP or URL from the alert story reveals which devices connected, when, and whether Microsoft threat intelligence or Defender Threat Intelligence knows it. From the same page an indicator can block the destination via network protection, which needs the Defender Antivirus network protection feature on. Exam clue: 'stop every device from reaching the command-and-control domain' is a domain indicator."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.3",q:"Alert page — alert story and Manage alert",a:"#Alert story is the process tree with every related entity clickable\nManage alert sets status (New, In progress, Resolved), classification, assignee and comments",s:"Process-tree evidence plus triage controls on the alert",
  x:"The alert page is where tier-1 works before escalating to the incident: expand the story to see parent processes and files, use the ellipsis on an entity to act, then update the Manage alert pane. Alerts can be linked to another incident or moved. On the exam, 'resolve the alert as a false positive and prevent the same alert for this scheduled task' combines Manage alert with Tune alert."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.3",q:"Alert classification and determination in Defender XDR",a:"#True positive, Informational expected activity, False positive, each with a determination\nTrue positive determinations include Malware, Phishing, Compromised account, Multistage attack",s:"Three classifications with specific determination reasons",g:"xdrclass",
  x:"Unlike Sentinel's four-way classification, Defender XDR uses three classifications and a determination list: True positive (multistage attack, malicious user activity, compromised account, malware, phishing, unwanted software, other), Informational expected activity (security testing, line-of-business application, confirmed activity, other), and False positive (not malicious, not enough data to validate, other). The exam may show a red-team scenario and expect Informational with Security testing.",
  w:"Defender: three classes plus a reason; Sentinel: four classes."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.3",q:"Tune alert — hide, resolve or set as behavior",a:"#Alert tuning rules match evidence conditions and then hide the alert, auto-resolve it, or convert it to a behavior\nHide prevents incident creation (Defender for Endpoint alerts only)",s:"Condition rule that hides, resolves or demotes repeat alerts",
  x:"Created from the alert page or Settings, a tuning rule names evidence such as a file hash, command line, process or scheduled task, with AND/OR grouping, and applies to future alerts matching those conditions. Set as behavior keeps the signal hunting-visible in the behaviors tables without an alert. Exam clue: 'the backup agent's script triggers the same alert daily' is a tuning rule, not a Defender for Cloud suppression rule."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.3",q:"Collect file and download file for evidence",a:"#Collect file asks the sensor to upload the sample; Download file retrieves it from the portal\nNeeded before deep analysis or offline reverse engineering",s:"Pull the sample into the portal, then download it",
  x:"Not every observed file is stored by Microsoft; if the file page lacks a sample, Collect file requests it from a device where it exists, after which Download file (password-protected archive) and Submit for deep analysis become available. The alternative is getfile in a live response session. Exam clue: 'deep analysis is greyed out' means the sample has not been collected yet."},
 /* 2.2.4 attack disruption */
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.4",q:"Automatic attack disruption — what it is",a:"#High-confidence, cross-signal containment that acts before an analyst does\nIncident gets an Attack Disruption tag and a yellow banner listing the actions",s:"Automatic containment on high-confidence incidents",
  x:"Attack disruption correlates endpoint, identity, email and SaaS signals into an incident with very high confidence, then contains the assets the attacker is using so lateral movement and remote encryption stop. It targets scenarios such as human-operated ransomware and business email compromise. The security team retains control because every action appears in the Action center and can be undone. Exam clue: 'the account was disabled before anyone in the SOC looked at the incident'.",
  w:"Disruption acts first; the SOC reviews and releases."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.4",q:"Contain device vs isolate device under attack disruption",a:"#Contain device: every onboarded device blocks traffic from the suspicious host, even if it is not onboarded\nIsolate device: the compromised onboarded device itself is cut off",s:"Block a rogue host's traffic versus cut off an onboarded one",g:"disruptact",
  x:"Containment works from the outside in: a policy pushed to all onboarded devices refuses communication from the contained device or IP, which is how Defender stops an unmanaged attacker machine it cannot isolate. Isolation works on the compromised device directly. Contain IP is the variant for undiscovered devices. Exam clue: 'the attacker's laptop is not onboarded' means contain device or contain IP."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.4",q:"Contain user under attack disruption",a:"#Temporarily blocks a suspicious identity's authentication, file and network access on all onboarded devices\nComplements Disable user (AD via Defender for Identity) and Suspend user (Entra ID)",s:"Endpoint-enforced block on a suspicious account",g:"disruptact",
  x:"Contain user does not touch the directory; it tells every onboarded device to reject that account's SMB, RDP and other remote activity, stopping lateral movement while the account still technically exists. Disable user is the directory-side action executed by the Defender for Identity sensor on a domain controller, and Suspend user in Entra ID and Revoke user session are the cloud equivalents. Exam items ask which action is taken by which product.",
  w:"Contain = endpoints refuse the user; disable = the directory refuses."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.4",q:"Where to review and undo attack disruption actions",a:"#Action center History lists each automatic action; undo or release from the device/user page\nIncident Activities tab shows policy status with provider Attack disruption",s:"Action center and the incident Activities tab",g:"disruptact",
  x:"After confirming that an account was cleaned or a device rebuilt, the analyst releases containment from the entity page or undoes the action from the Action center, and the incident banner updates. The Activities tab filter Provider: Attack disruption with Policy status Active, Inactive or No status shows what is still enforced. Exam clue: 'the user still cannot sign in after remediation' means a disruption action is still active and must be undone."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.4",q:"Excluding assets from automatic attack disruption",a:"#Exclusions can be set for specific users, devices and IP addresses\nUsed for critical infrastructure accounts that must never be auto-contained",s:"Opt-out list of users, devices and IPs",
  x:"Attack disruption is on by default for eligible tenants, and Microsoft recommends leaving it on, but domain controllers, break-glass accounts and service accounts for backups may be excluded so an automatic disable does not take down production. Exclusions are configured in the Defender settings for automated response. On the exam, 'ensure the SQL service account is never disabled automatically' is an exclusion, not a tuning rule."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.4",q:"Prerequisites for attack disruption actions",a:"#Device actions need Defender for Endpoint; user disable needs Defender for Identity with action accounts\nEntra actions need the Entra integration; exclusions and automation level must allow it",s:"Each product must be deployed for its own disruption action",
  x:"Disruption can only act through the products that are in place: no Defender for Identity sensor on a DC means no automatic Disable user in AD, and no onboarded devices means no contain device. Device groups set to no automated response also block it. Exam stems describe a tenant with Defender for Endpoint but no Defender for Identity and ask why only the device was contained."},
 {c:"dferesp",obj:"2.2.4",q:"Attack disruption vs automated investigation and response",a:"#Disruption: immediate containment of attacker assets on high-confidence incidents, no approval\nAIR: evidence-by-evidence investigation with remediations that usually await approval",s:"Instant containment versus investigate-then-approve",
  x:"Both are automatic but they answer different questions. AIR asks 'is this file or email malicious?' and queues a remediation per entity according to the device group's automation level. Attack disruption asks 'is this an active attack?' and contains the user or device as a whole. The exam tests the approval difference: AIR actions sit in Pending; disruption actions are already done and sit in History.",
  w:"AIR waits for a thumbs-up; disruption asks forgiveness."},
 /* ---------- m365inv (24) ---------- */
 /* 2.3.1 Purview Audit */
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"Audit (Standard) — retention and scope",a:"#180 days of retention, on by default for eligible tenants\nNo retention policies, no high-value events",s:"Default 180-day log without premium events",g:"audittier",
  x:"Audit (Standard) records user and admin activity across Exchange, SharePoint, OneDrive, Teams, Entra and more, searchable in the Purview portal, via Search-UnifiedAuditLog and the Management Activity API. Retention moved from 90 to 180 days in October 2023 and cannot be extended at this tier. Exam stem: 'the investigation needs events from eight months ago on an E3 tenant' is a Standard limitation.",
  w:"Standard = 180 days, no knobs."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"Audit (Premium) — retention and extras",a:"#One year by default for Exchange, SharePoint and Entra records; up to ten years with the add-on\nAdds retention policies and high-value events such as MailItemsAccessed",s:"One-year default, ten-year add-on, intelligent insights",g:"audittier",
  x:"Premium comes with E5-class licensing and keeps Exchange, SharePoint and Entra audit records for a year (other workloads stay at 180 days) with a per-user 10-Year Audit Log Retention add-on available. Audit log retention policies can lengthen retention per workload, activity or user. It also roughly doubles Management Activity API bandwidth. Exam clue: 'retain audit records for seven years for regulators' is Premium plus the add-on and a retention policy.",
  w:"Premium = a year by default, a decade with the add-on."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"MailItemsAccessed — why it matters",a:"#Premium audit event recording that a mailbox item was read or bound to\nKey evidence for scoping a mailbox compromise",s:"Premium event showing which mail items were accessed",
  x:"Without MailItemsAccessed an investigator knows an attacker signed in but not which messages they opened; with it, each sync or bind operation is logged with the session and client. Other premium events include Send and the Teams message events. Exam clue: 'prove which emails the attacker read after the account takeover' requires Audit (Premium) and this record type.",
  w:"MailItemsAccessed = what they read, not just that they logged in."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"Audit search in the Purview portal — filters",a:"#Date range (UTC, max 180 days), activities by friendly or operation name, users, file/folder/site, record types, workloads, keyword\nAdmin units scope results for restricted admins",s:"Date, activity, user, object, record type and keyword",
  x:"Searches run as asynchronous jobs that keep going if you close the browser, up to ten in parallel per account (only one unfiltered), and finished jobs are kept for 30 days. Results can be exported to CSV (50,000 rows on Standard, up to a million on Premium). Exchange, SharePoint and Teams events typically appear within 60 to 90 minutes. Exam clue: 'find who downloaded files from a SharePoint site last week' is this tool."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"Roles required to search the audit log",a:"#Audit Logs role or View-Only Audit Logs role in Purview\nAssigned through role groups such as Audit Manager or Audit Reader",s:"Audit Logs or View-Only Audit Logs role",
  x:"Audit search is not open to every security reader. The View-Only Audit Logs role suffices for searching and exporting; the Audit Logs role adds the ability to manage audit configuration such as retention policies. Restricted administrators can only see events inside their administrative units. On the exam, 'the analyst gets an access denied message in audit search' is answered by assigning one of these roles."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"Search-UnifiedAuditLog — key parameters",a:"#StartDate and EndDate are required; filter with UserIds, Operations, RecordType, ObjectIds, IPAddresses, FreeText\nDefault 100 results; ResultSize up to 5,000; SessionCommand ReturnLargeSet up to 50,000",s:"Exchange Online PowerShell cmdlet with date range filters",k:"Search-UnifiedAuditLog -StartDate 09/01/2026 -EndDate 09/08/2026 `\n  -UserIds ana@fabrikam.com -Operations MailItemsAccessed `\n  -SessionId \"ir-0421\" -SessionCommand ReturnLargeSet",
  x:"The cmdlet runs in Exchange Online PowerShell and returns records whose details live in the AuditData JSON property, which you parse with ConvertFrom-Json. Large pulls use a SessionId and ReturnLargeSet, re-running the same command to page through up to 50,000 records. Microsoft steers production integrations to the Management Activity API. Exam clue: 'script the export of one user's mailbox activity' is this cmdlet."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"Audit log retention policies",a:"#Premium feature that sets retention per record type, activity or user, up to ten years\nUseful for keeping privileged-user or Exchange events longer than the default",s:"Per-workload or per-user retention overrides on Premium",
  x:"A retention policy has a name, priority, the record types or activities it covers, optional users, and a duration. Higher-priority policies win when several match. They do not shorten retention below the default. Because they are Premium-only, the exam uses them to test licensing: an E3 tenant asked to keep SharePoint events for two years must first obtain Premium."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"Delay before audit events are searchable",a:"#Most Exchange, SharePoint, OneDrive and Teams events appear within 60 to 90 minutes\nOther workloads may take longer; Microsoft publishes no SLA",s:"Roughly an hour to ninety minutes for the main workloads",
  x:"Audit records are collected from each service and written to the unified log asynchronously, so an investigation launched minutes after an incident may find nothing yet. The right move is to wait or use real-time sources such as Defender advanced hunting. Exam clue: 'the activity happened ten minutes ago and audit search returns nothing' is latency, not a missing license."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"Audit search job behaviour",a:"#Jobs run asynchronously, up to 10 per admin at once, kept for 30 days, and can be named\nLarge-tenant jobs can take up to 48 hours",s:"Named asynchronous jobs retained for thirty days",
  x:"Because searches are jobs, an analyst can start several scoped queries, close the browser and return to the results list. Only one unfiltered job may run at a time, so a broad 'everything for 180 days' search blocks a second broad one. Export runs from the completed job. On the exam, 'the search seems stuck' usually means a large unfiltered job; narrow the activity and user filters."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.1",q:"Audit vs Defender advanced hunting for M365 activity",a:"#Purview Audit: authoritative, long-retained, compliance-grade record of all workloads\nAdvanced hunting CloudAppEvents: 30 days, KQL, joinable with device and email tables",s:"Authoritative long-term log versus 30-day KQL-joinable copy",
  x:"CloudAppEvents in Defender XDR mirrors much of the Office 365 audit stream for 30 days and is the faster tool for correlating a SharePoint download with a device alert. Purview Audit is the system of record with 180-day or longer retention, export and legal defensibility. Exam clue: 'correlate the file download with the endpoint process that performed it' is advanced hunting; 'produce a record for legal' is Audit."},
 /* 2.3.2 eDiscovery content search */
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.2",q:"Content search in the new Purview eDiscovery — where it lives",a:"#Searches are created inside an eDiscovery case in the Purview portal\nClassic Content search and classic Standard and Premium retired August 31, 2025",s:"Case-based search in the unified eDiscovery experience",
  x:"The old standalone Content search page no longer exists for most tenants; investigators create a case and then a search within it, using the query builder or KQL editor against mailboxes, OneDrive, SharePoint sites, Teams and Microsoft 365 groups. The exam has moved with the product, so 'create a search to find messages with a subject' expects the unified experience. 21Vianet tenants are the only exception."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.2",q:"Building a content search query",a:"#Keywords and KQL with properties such as from, subject, sent, filetype\nConditions narrow by date, sender, participants, file type or sensitivity label",s:"KQL keywords plus condition cards",k:"subject:\"invoice\" AND from:ana@fabrikam.com AND sent>=2026-09-01",
  x:"Search queries use Keyword Query Language, so AND, OR, NOT, parentheses and property restrictions work. Conditions are added as cards that AND with the keywords. A common exam pattern is a question that lists a goal (find all messages with an attachment of a certain name sent in a window) and asks for the query or condition set. Statistics let you estimate size before exporting."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.2",q:"Search locations in eDiscovery",a:"#Exchange mailboxes (including group and Teams mailboxes), SharePoint and OneDrive sites, Microsoft 365 groups\nCan target specific users or all locations",s:"Mailboxes, sites, OneDrive and groups as data sources",
  x:"Teams chats are stored in user mailboxes and channel messages in group mailboxes, so a Teams investigation selects those mailboxes and the team's SharePoint site. Searching all mailboxes is possible but slow and may hit result limits, so investigators scope by custodian. On the exam, 'find the Teams message a user sent in a private chat' means search the user's mailbox."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.2",q:"Statistics, sample and export on a search",a:"#Generate statistics shows item counts, size and locations; sample previews a subset\nExport delivers results for download; Premium can add them to a review set instead",s:"Estimate, preview, then export or review",
  x:"Running statistics first lets you refine the query without pulling data; the sample shows real items for a sanity check. Export produces downloadable content (the separate eDiscovery Export tool retired in 2025). Premium's review set adds culling, tagging and redaction. Exam clue: 'confirm the query scope before exporting gigabytes' is statistics and sample."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.2",q:"eDiscovery holds",a:"#Preserve content in selected locations so deletion does not remove it from search\nFull hold on a location or query-based hold on matching items",s:"Preservation on locations or on query matches",
  x:"A hold does not stop the user working; it keeps copies of deleted or edited items in Recoverable Items or the preservation hold library until the hold is released. Investigators place a hold at the start of a case so a suspect cannot destroy evidence, then search the held locations. On the exam, 'ensure the user cannot delete the messages before legal reviews them' is a hold, not a retention label."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.2",q:"eDiscovery Standard vs Premium",a:"#Standard: cases, searches, holds, export\nPremium: adds custodians, review sets, analytics, communications and EDRM-style workflow",s:"Search-and-export cases versus full review workflow",
  x:"Standard covers the typical SOC investigation: scope, search, preserve and export. Premium is aimed at legal matters and adds custodian management, legal hold notifications, review sets stored in Azure, near-duplicate detection, tagging and redaction, with Insider Risk cases able to escalate into Premium. The exam tests licensing: review sets require Premium (E5), search and export do not."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.2",q:"eDiscovery Manager vs eDiscovery Administrator",a:"#Both are subgroups of the eDiscovery Manager role group\nManager works only cases they are a member of; Administrator can access every case and manage members",s:"Case member versus all-cases administrator",
  x:"Adding a user to the eDiscovery Manager role group grants search, hold and export rights, but a Manager only sees the cases they created or were added to. The eDiscovery Administrator subgroup sees all cases in the organisation and can add or remove members. Organization Management can also create Standard cases. Exam clue: 'an investigator cannot see a case created by a colleague' means they are a Manager, not an Administrator."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.2",q:"Content search vs Purview Audit — which tool when",a:"#Content search finds the content of messages and files\nAudit finds the activity around them: who accessed, shared, deleted",s:"Find the message versus find who touched it",
  x:"Investigators use both in sequence: Audit shows that an attacker ran an inbox rule and forwarded mail, then a content search retrieves the forwarded messages for review. A stem that asks for the text of a phishing email delivered to 40 users is a content search; a stem that asks who opened it is Audit (with MailItemsAccessed on Premium)."},
 /* 2.3.3 Graph activity logs */
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.3",q:"Microsoft Graph activity logs — what they contain",a:"#An audit trail of every HTTP request to Microsoft Graph for the tenant\nApps, SDKs, AI clients and Microsoft first-party apps alike",s:"Per-request log of Graph API calls into the tenant",
  x:"Sign-in logs tell you a token was issued; Graph activity logs tell you what the token was then used for: which endpoints were called, by which app and user, from which IP and with what response code. That makes them the primary source for token-theft and malicious OAuth app investigations. Exam clue: 'determine which mailboxes an abused application enumerated through the API' is this log."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.3",q:"Enabling Graph activity logs and where they land",a:"#Diagnostic settings on Microsoft Entra ID routed to Log Analytics, Storage or Event Hubs\nTable name in Log Analytics: MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs",s:"Entra diagnostic setting to a workspace table",g:"graphlog",
  x:"The log is a category in the Entra ID diagnostic settings blade, like SignInLogs and AuditLogs, so it needs an Azure subscription and a destination. Log Analytics is the recommended target for KQL analysis and Sentinel; Event Hubs feeds third-party SIEMs. Ingestion cannot be filtered in the diagnostic setting, so volume is controlled by workspace transformations. Exam clue: 'the table is empty' usually means the diagnostic setting was never created."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.3",q:"Licensing and role to turn on Graph activity logs",a:"#Microsoft Entra ID P1 or P2 license on the tenant\nSecurity Administrator or higher to configure the diagnostic setting",s:"P1/P2 tenant plus Security Administrator",g:"graphlog",
  x:"Collection is gated on a premium Entra license, and the person creating the diagnostic setting needs an Entra administrator role plus permissions on the Azure destination resources. Delivery latency is typically under 30 minutes but can reach two hours. On the exam, a Free-tier tenant asking why Graph activity logs are unavailable is a licensing question, not a permissions one."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.3",q:"Key columns in MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs",a:"#RequestUri, RequestMethod, ResponseStatusCode, UserId, ServicePrincipalId, AppId, IPAddress\nAlso DurationMs, ResponseSizeBytes, ClientAuthMethod, TimeGenerated",s:"URI, method, status, user, app, service principal and IP",g:"graphlog",k:"MicrosoftGraphActivityLogs\n| where AppId == \"<suspect-app-id>\"\n| summarize count() by RequestUri, ResponseStatusCode, IPAddress",
  x:"A hunt for an abused app groups requests by AppId and RequestUri to see whether it walked /users, /me/messages or /groups, then checks ResponseStatusCode for 200 versus 403 or 429 throttling. UserId identifies delegated calls; ServicePrincipalId with an empty UserId identifies application-only calls. ClientAuthMethod shows whether a secret or certificate was used. Exam items present a few rows and ask what the app was doing."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.3",q:"Graph activity logs vs Entra sign-in logs",a:"#Sign-in logs: authentication events and token issuance\nGraph activity logs: the API calls made with those tokens afterwards",s:"Who signed in versus what the token was used for",
  x:"The two are complementary and are commonly joined on the user or app identifier to build a full story: a risky sign-in followed by hundreds of /users requests is a reconnaissance pattern. Sign-in logs exist on every tenant; Graph activity logs need P1 or P2 and a diagnostic setting. Exam clue: 'the sign-in looked normal but data was exfiltrated via the API' is only visible in the Graph activity log."},
 {c:"m365inv",obj:"2.3.3",q:"Sensitive data caveat in Graph activity logs",a:"#Request details are logged as sent; secrets or tokens placed in a URI become visible to workspace readers\nMulti-tenant apps only see their own tenant's calls",s:"Logged requests can expose secrets passed in URIs",
  x:"Because the log captures the request URI and metadata, any poorly built app that passes credentials in query strings leaks them into the workspace, so access to the table should be limited and reviewed. The log also cannot show activity in other tenants even for a multi-tenant app. The exam uses this for a least-privilege question about who should be able to read the table."},
];
